 Vodafone Idea 
 Jefferies initiates BUY on VIL with Rs 20 target.
 VIL revenue CAGR expected at 11% (FY26-29).
 Turnaround depends on subscriber stabilization, capital.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
Vodafone Idea (VIL) offers a high-beta turnaround opportunity in Indian telecom, with subscriber stabilisation expected to support an 11% revenue CAGR over FY26-29 and, together with strong operating leverage, drive a 25% cash EBITDA CAGR and a sharp ROIC inflection through FY31, according to Jefferies.
The brokerage, which has initiated coverage on VIL with a BUY rating and a Rs 20 price target, expects the company's premium valuation multiples to sustain during the turnaround. It estimates that every 10% tariff hike could potentially drive around 34% upside in VIL's equity value.
The turnaround thesis hinges on stabilising the subscriber base following sustained network investments. VIL's subscriber churn has declined from 113 million in FY23 to 98 million in FY26, and Jefferies expects further network investments to reduce churn and improve gross additions. This is expected to result in 4-13 million net subscriber additions over FY27-29.
A stabilising subscriber base should also allow VIL to capture a greater share of future tariff increases in revenue. Jefferies expects mobile revenues to grow at a 12% CAGR over FY26-29, supported by a 9% CAGR in ARPU and 3% subscriber growth. Its estimates factor in a 15% tariff hike in April 2027.
Jefferies expects revenue growth to translate into significantly faster earnings growth because of VIL's operating leverage. Cash EBITDA is projected to grow at a 25% CAGR over FY26-29, compared with 14% for Bharti Airtel's India business, while VIL's cash EBITDA margin is expected to expand by around 840 basis points over FY26-29.
The brokerage also sees cash EBITDA continuing to grow at around 25% annually through FY31, with incremental EBITDA margins rising above 60% beyond FY29 as accelerated network rollouts come to an end. This is expected to drive a sharp improvement in return on invested capital, from negative levels currently to double digits by FY32.
However, the turnaround will require continued access to capital. Jefferies expects VIL's cash outflows to rise above Rs 40,000 crore annually during FY29-34, creating a temporary cash-flow mismatch despite steady growth in operating cash flows. A Rs 25,000 crore debt raise is expected to bridge the FY27-29 shortfall, while the company would require a further Rs 16,000 crore equity infusion in FY30.
The FY30 equity infusion would also trigger a potential Rs 15,300 crore conversion of spectrum liabilities into equity by the government, which Jefferies believes would be sufficient to address the cash-flow shortfall through FY34.
Jefferies believes raising the additional equity should be manageable given VIL's history of capital raising and continued government support. The company has raised Rs 44,700 crore of fresh equity since 2019, including Rs 26,700 crore from its promoters.
The brokerage therefore sees VIL as a high-beta play on India's telecom tariff cycle, with the combination of subscriber stabilisation, premiumisation, tariff hikes and operating leverage offering significant upside if the turnaround executes as expected.
 Danish Khan is the editor of Technology and Telecom. He was previously with the Economic Times and has tracked the sector for 15 years. 
